ompeting views um when you look on  the internet from people that are r  kelly supporters who who think that  they're just killing it and and some of  the for lack of a better term mainstream  media when you read those accounts you  think that the case is problematic for  them although in both accounts there's  still these weaknesses in the ricoh case  i don't agree with the approach of some  of the attorneys i think that some of  the attorneys in the case smartly uh  have now been made to sit down because  they have no idea what they're doing do  you think that these 12 people that have  been selected  so far  are gonna carry the day for r kelly do  you think they're gonna lean more  towards the prosecution  well and and here's the problem for our  kelly in a rico case in order to prove  the rico they only need two  substantive acts to be proven  so  the jurors only have to agree that two  of them many many things i think there's  18 separate acts occurred they could  discount everything but for instance the  couple of man activations which are  really the easiest ones for the  government to prove and that's why you  have to attack the enterprise because  then you need all 12 people i think all  12 people are going to agree that  there's a couple of predicate acts but  then you need them to agree on the  existence of the enterprise but i don't  think that you're going to be able to  get 12 people to agree that there was in  fact an enterprise here  let's bring back former federal  prosecutor gene rossi and terry austin  terry a former employee on the stand  from friday said that r kelly asked her  why she let the women escape could that  spell out rico against kelly  absolutely and the operable word here  brian is escape there are over a dozen  underlying acts for the ricoh charge and  one of them is kidnapping and forced  labor and if kelly himself used that  word escape it means he knows he's  holding people against their will he's  forcing them to do labor against their  will so her testimony was absolutely  critical here  gene i get the technical argument that  steve greenberg is is making here but it  begs the question what's the difference  between an enterprise for sex  trafficking and people working for  someone who happens to be a sexual  predator  well one is uh conspiracy uh i can  conspire with somebody say we had a  lemonade stand instead of lemonade we  sold moonshine and we're just using a  chair in front of a house and two or  three of us are selling moonshine that's  a conspiracy  what mr greenberg rightly says is uh  enterprise is much different it has an  infrastructure it can be formal or  informal and what he's saying is there's  no infrastructure uh by which these  crimes were committed that's a tough  road to hope given the predicate acts  that have been alleged all right well  gene you let me know where this lemonade  stand is we're definitely gonna check it  out later  thank you for that  still ahead on law and crime daily after  four months of back and forth closing  arguments are underway in the trial  against robert durst we'll hear from the  real estate airs defense first hand plus  big news out of wisconsin in connection  to the slenderman stabbings where a  defendant is headed after five years in  a state mental health institution  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily  covering cases from coast to coast we  begin in wisconsin where a defendant in  the so-called slender man stabbing is  said to be released from a mental health  institution  nineteen-year-old anissa weir and morgan  geyser were convicted back in 2014 of  stabbing their middle school classmates  19 times all this they said was the name  of slenderman a fictional character that  appeared in fan fiction and video games  the girls lured then 14 year old payton  lightner into the woods stabbed her and  left her for dead lightner survived the  near deadly attack  in 2017 weir was found not guilty by  mental disease or defect and